# Threading & Jobs

Burst and Unity Jobs are core to this project. Default to off-main-thread execution.

## Main Thread Rule

The main thread (`ThreadRules.MainThread`) is **only** for work that cannot run elsewhere: UnityEngine/MonoBehaviour lifecycle, rendering hooks, managed-only APIs, and sync points that bridge Jobs back to Unity objects.

**Player input** is read and processed on the client main thread. The result (position, direction, speed) is queued as a command for the server — raw input never crosses the network.

Everything else belongs in a `ThreadRules` phase:

- **MainJob** — command-stream processing, server-side entity create/destroy
- **AiSystemUpdateJob**, **MeshBuilderJob**, **NavmeshBuilderJob** — read/write pre-allocated buffers only
- **NetworkJob** — network I/O; writes to `EventsStream.Writer` and pre-allocated buffers
- **SyncPhase** — job start/end synchronization (runs after MainJob syncs with main thread)
- **FileIoTask** — disk reads into pre-allocated buffers

Respect `<remarks>Access only from ThreadRules.X</remarks>` on types (e.g. `Storage` → MainJob, `Storage.Managed` → SyncPhase).

## Jobs & Burst

- Mark hot paths with `[BurstCompile]`; keep job structs `unmanaged`.
- Use `NativeArray` / project allocators (`SessionRewindableAllocator`, pools) — avoid managed allocations inside Jobs.
- Job threads read/write **pre-allocated buffers**, not shared managed state.
- Schedule work in the appropriate phase; do not bypass `SyncPhase` when cross-phase data is involved.

## Anti-patterns

```csharp
// ❌ Heavy logic or game-state mutation on main thread
void Update() { SimulateAllEntities(); }

// ❌ Managed heap allocation inside a Job
[BurstCompile] struct BadJob : IJob { public void Execute() { new List<int>(); } }

// ✅ Process commands in MainJob, sync via SyncPhase, render on main thread
```
